The generation of vorticity along the blades is obtained through an immersed lifting line approach. The lifting-line theory, developed by Prandtl in 1918, defines a mathematical model that permits to estimate the lift distribution of a threedimensional blade based on its geometry. This model shed a vortex-sheet along the whole bladespan from the trailing edge instead of only taking into account the blade tip vortex. The approach relies on the Kutta-Joukowski theorem that relates the lift production to the relative flow Vrel and the distribution of the circulation Γ over the span
